    "https://careers.k-state.edu/jobs/faculty-position-in-interior-design-manhattan-kansas-united-states": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Interior Design",
      "description": "Faculty Position in Interior Design - Manhattan, Kansas, United States Faculty Position in Interior Design - Manhattan, Kansas, United States Kiosk mode Faculty Position in Interior Design Apply Now Faculty Position in Interior Design Teach courses across a CIDA-accredited interior design curriculum, co-leading studios integrating current practice methods and digital technologies, with a strong emphasis on collaboration, human-centered and evidence-based design and innovation. 522185 Manhattan 3670015040 Department of Design Academic / Faculty - 9 month Closing at: Jan 5 2027 - 23:55 CST On-site Add to favorites View favorites About This Role The Interior Design program at Kansas State University invites applications for a 9-month faculty position beginning August 2027. The successful candidate may be appointed as either a tenure-track Assistant Professor or Professor of Practice, depending on qualifications and professional experience. Salary will be competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Our program is distinguished by the scholarly and professional expertise of its faculty and a shared commitment to preparing entry-level interior design professionals. Our curriculum emphasizes collaboration, human-centered and evidence-based design, technical competency, and design approaches that promote health, well-being, and responsible professional practice. We seek a collaborative, future-oriented colleague who brings creative energy, professional expertise, and a commitment to interior design education. Candidates should demonstrate the ability to contribute to a team-based teaching environment and to integrate contemporary professional practice into the curriculum. SHARED TEACHING RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will teach design studios and other courses across the Interior Design curriculum. Candidates should be prepared to integrate contemporary methods and technologies into studio instruction and demonstrate proficiency with software commonly used in professional practice, including Revit, Enscape, and Adobe Creative Cloud. Candidates should demonstrate the ability to teach in design studios and in two or more of the following curricular areas: evidence-based design; history and theory of interior design; materials and construction; textiles; construction documents; building systems and technology; and digital visualization. Studio courses are commonly co-taught. Candidates should therefore demonstrate collaborative professional and/or teaching experience and an ability to contribute effectively to shared course leadership, curriculum coordination, and student learning. APPOINTMENT PATHWAYS TENURE-TRACK ASSISTANT PROFESSOR The tenure-track appointment supports the university's integrated mission of teaching, research/creative scholarship, and service. Teaching — 65–70% Teach undergraduate interior design studios and other courses aligned with the candidate's expertise and program needs. Contribute to collaborative teaching, curriculum development, and continuous improvement of the Interior Design program. Research and Creative Scholarship — 25–30% Develop and sustain a focused program of research and/or creative scholarship informed by the candidate's academic preparation, professional expertise, teaching interests, or emerging areas of inquiry. The successful candidate's scholarly and creative work will contribute to advancement within the discipline and form an important basis for tenure and promotion. Service — 5–10% Contribute to departmental governance; college and university committees and initiatives; student, professional, and community engagement; and relevant disciplinary or professional organizations. PROFESSOR OF PRACTICE The Professor of Practice appointment emphasizes teaching excellence, professional expertise, and engagement with the Interior Design program and profession. Teaching — 80–90% Teach undergraduate interior design studios and other courses aligned with the candidate's professional expertise and program needs. Bring substantial professional practice experience into the classroom and contribute to collaborative teaching, curriculum development, and the integration of current professional standards, technologies, and methods. Service — 10–20% Contribute to departmental governance, program and college initiatives, student engagement, and professional communities. The successful candidate will help strengthen connections among the program, alumni, practitioners, and the broader interior design profession. About Us The Interior Design program at Kansas State University offers a four-year, first-professional Bachelor of Science in Interior Design. The program is part of the Department of Interior Architecture and Industrial Design (IAID) within the College of Architecture, Planning and Design (APDesign). In 2025, Interior Design celebrated 100 years of design education at Kansas State University. The program enrolls approximately 137 students and is supported by five full-time faculty. It is accredited by the Council for Interior Design Accreditation (CIDA), and Kansas State University is an accredited institutional member of the National Association of Schools of Art and Design (NASAD). Our students have earned recognition through national and international design competitions and are valued by employers for their professional preparation, collaborative abilities, and workplace readiness. The 2025 graduating class achieved 100% employment in the field by January 1, 2026. As part of APDesign, Interior Design faculty and students have opportunities to collaborate with programs in Interior Architecture, Industrial Design, Architecture, Landscape Architecture, and Regional & Community Planning. APDesign is housed in the Seaton and Regnier Hall complex, which provides contemporary teaching, research, and collaborative spaces and approximately 20,000 square feet of fabrication and research laboratories. The Interior Design faculty approach design education as a collaborative endeavor. Faculty co-teach studios, coordinate learning across the curriculum, and continually evaluate courses and pedagogical approaches to respond to changes in professional practice, technology, research, and societal needs. We seek a colleague who will contribute to this culture while bringing new perspectives and expertise to the program. Our curriculum prepares interior designers to address complex human and societal needs through evidence-based, human-centered design while meeting professional responsibilities and client expectations. We value intellectual diversity, collaboration, innovation, ethical professional practice, and the role of design in supporting human health and well-being. Additional information may be found at www.ksu.edu (university), apdesign.k-state.edu (college),apdesign.k-state.edu/IAID (department), and www.manhattan.org (community). Worksite Description This position is On-site . What You'll Need to Succeed Minimum Qualifications: FOR TENURE-TRACK ASSISTANT PROFESSOR Master's degree or higher in Interior Design, Interior Architecture, Architecture, or a closely related field. Minimum of three years of professional practice experience in interior design, interior architecture, architecture, or a closely related field and qualified for NCIDQ examination. Demonstrated excellence in written, oral, and visual communication appropriate to the design disciplines. Demonstrated proficiency with contemporary digital design and visualization tools used in professional practice, including Revit and Adobe Creative Cloud, with experience in Enscape, AutoCAD, and/or comparable platforms. Demonstrated ability to work effectively in collaborative professional and/or teaching environments. Demonstrated potential to establish and sustain a scholarly and/or creative agenda related to the candidate's academic preparation, professional expertise, or areas of design inquiry. FOR PROFESSOR OF PRACTICE Bachelor's degree or higher in Interior Design, Interior Architecture, Architecture, or a closely related field. Minimum of 10 years of professional practice experience in interior design, interior architecture, architecture, or a closely related field. Current NCIDQ certification. Demonstrated record of professional accomplishment and leadership appropriate to a practice-based faculty appointment. Demonstrated excellence in written, oral, and visual communication appropriate to the design disciplines. Demonstrated proficiency with contemporary digital design and visualization tools used in professional practice, including Revit and Adobe Creative Cloud, with experience in Enscape, AutoCAD, and/or comparable platforms. Demonstrated ability to work effectively in collaborative professional and/or teaching environments. The degree requirement is necessary for accreditation purposes and to ensure candidates possess the specialized knowledge, skills, and abilities that can only be attained through a formal educational program at this level. Preferred Qualifications: FOR TENURE-TRACK ASSISTANT PROFESSOR PhD or other terminal degree in Interior Design or a related field. More than three years of professional practice experience. NCIDQ certification and/or architectural licensure. Professional certifications or credentials relevant to the discipline, such as WELL, EDAC, LEED, or NKBA credentials. Experience teaching undergraduate design studios, including collaborative or co-teaching environments. Ability to develop and teach courses in multiple areas of the required interior design curriculum. Evidence of an emerging or established record of research, scholarship, and/or creative activity. Record of service to academic and/or professional communities. FOR PROFESSOR OF PRACTICE 15 or more years of professional practice experience. Master's degree in Interior Design, Interior Architecture, Architecture, or a closely related field. Architectural licensure and/or additional professional certifications relevant to the discipline, such as WELL, EDAC, LEED, or NKBA credentials. Evidence of leadership in project delivery, design practice, firm leadership, or other areas relevant to interior design education. Previous teaching, mentoring, professional training, or studio instruction experience. Demonstrated ability to teach multiple levels of undergraduate design studio and contribute to co-teaching leadership. Ability to develop and teach courses in multiple areas of the required interior design curriculum. Record of leadership or service within professional organizations and/or the broader design community. Sponsorship eligibility: Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the U.S. on an ongoing basis without sponsorship How to Apply Please submit the following documents: Letter of interest addressing qualifications. Curriculum vitae. Professional portfolio. Teaching portfolio (if taught previously). List of three academic and/or professional references with full contact information. Application Window Applications close on: January 5, 2027 Anticipated Hiring Pay Range $70,000-$78,000 Sign up for job alerts First Name (required) c71f9b4e Last Name (required) 89ecfe94 Email (required) 32729122 Select one or more from the lists below to receive information about specific jobs: c0d78f94 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Education / Instructional Education / Instructional Employment Type Academic / Faculty - 9 month Academic / Faculty - 9 month Locations Manhattan, Kansas, United States Manhattan, Kansas, United States Worksite On-site On-site Sign Up! Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Save this job for later Add this job to your favorites or send it to yourself to apply later Save this job to your favorites or send it to 